Z||000000||ezsurf||03-29-2000||03:58 PM||||Ogre, your article has some excellent info on the cooling system.  However, I was wondering if the article was written specifically for the 4 cylinder engine. Is the 6 cylinder system exactly the same?  I believe there was a single speed fan on some systems and a two speed fan on others.  This may not make any difference, but I thought I would ask. -  ez||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||03-29-2000||04:44 PM||||Where is the article?||||reg||1
Z||000002||ezsurf||03-29-2000||08:29 PM||||Go to Pennock's main menu and look in Ogre's cave tunnel #6.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-29-2000||08:45 PM||||Thanks, I wondered if anyone ever read any of that. I have slowly been working on more stuff for there.<P>While written based mainly on the 4 cyl, the data was checked against the V6 as well. The cooling system, other than fan, is pretty much the same for All Fieros. <P>The 2 speed fan is only used on early 4 cylinder cars. In these cars there are 3 fan temp switches. Low is 221F, High is 246, the third switch is connected parralel to the low switch and activates at 235F. No. It doesn't make much sense.<P>The single speed system switches at 235F and is the same for all Fiero. The only changes I know of from 4-6cyl are hose connections in the back.<P>I still believe that the fan should be locked on nearly full time when ambient air temp is above around 70-80F. This prevents the temperature bounce normally seen in thick traffic and at long intersetions.<P>What I would like to know is the value of the resistor used on the 2 speed fan. Knowing this would allow the single speed motor to replace the 2 speed motor. The 2 speed motor is listed as available in Wrenchead, but is around 200$us.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||ezsurf||03-29-2000||09:16 PM||||One of the previous owners of my car modified the fan so it runs continously.  I'm glad they did. I'm convinced this is the only thing that prevented a nuclear meltdown.  I wish I had a picture of the heads when I first pulled off the valve covers.  I have never seen anything like it. The carbon was so thick it looked like you packed the heads in bearing grease. What's even more unbelievable is how well the engine actually ran.  Oil could not even drain back to the pan.  It was being forced under pressure out the valve covers.  I would like to thank whoever did this mod.  It's about the only thing they did right.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eddie.H||03-29-2000||09:32 PM||||Ezsurf that fan running all the time is what most likely caused the carbon to get so thick in the frist place the motor may never got hot enough to lean out. or if its an 87/88 the ecm did not no the moror was warmed up and to a just for it. I think thats how it works ...... AM I right or wrong Theogre Eddie||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-30-2000||07:41 PM||||Eddie,<P>I've run the fan full time. The ECM doesn't care. (I've checked it with the ECM scanner in real time.)The engine takes only slightly longer to warm up, but it definatly warms up. The only times it won't properly warm up are:<P>the Thermostat is out<BR>The thermostat is jammed open<BR>a 160 thermostat has been installed without chippping the ECM. (Chips for 165 stats are only available for V6) I haven't tested a 160 stat on a 4. Acording to 4 cyl spec's it's right on the edge of working if it works. (((A 180 stat works just fine one the 4 & 6 cyl with no ECM changes)))||||reg||1
Z||000007||FIEROCIOUS||03-31-2000||12:31 AM||||Hey Ogre, I have a ADS chip will this work with a 160 thermostat? <BR>I ordered it from JC Whitney so I don't know what version it is. How could I find out if it's Street or Strip?<P>Will the computer go into open-loop with the ADS chip @ 160?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||03-31-2000||11:20 AM||||Ogre, I've put a link to your cave at the bottom of the page so peple can find it easier.||||reg||3
Z||000009||theogre||03-31-2000||10:20 PM||||Thanks Cliff.<P>FIEROCIOUS,<P>Don't know. Ask ADS. Hypertech has one that requires the 160 and the low temp fan switch.||||reg||1
